1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,108
These two commands appear in blog posts
under headings like "clean up your

2
00:00:09,148 --> 00:00:14,258
repository" and "make Git smaller". They're
also the documented way to destroy the

3
00:00:14,298 --> 00:00:19,032
safety net that the last six videos
relied on. After them, git reflog prints

4
00:00:19,072 --> 00:00:24,182
nothing, git fsck --lost-found prints
nothing, and a commit that was recoverable a

5
00:00:24,222 --> 00:00:25,752
minute ago doesn't exist.

6
00:00:26,092 --> 00:00:29,128
There is one situation in
which you want exactly that.

7
00:00:29,168 --> 00:00:33,627
A secret was committed and never pushed,
and it must not survive in that clone.

8
00:00:33,667 --> 00:00:38,240
And there's every other situation, where
someone runs the pair during an incident

9
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:41,372
and turns a five-minute
recovery into a permanent loss.

10
00:00:41,712 --> 00:00:46,673
A senior engineer has to know both: how to
reach the point of no return on purpose,

11
00:00:46,713 --> 00:00:49,746
and how to make sure nobody
reaches it by accident.

12
00:00:49,786 --> 00:00:54,687
One question to carry with you: after those
two commands, can anything still bring

13
00:00:54,727 --> 00:00:55,651
the commit back?

14
00:00:57,491 --> 00:01:01,185
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:01,225 --> 00:01:04,491
This is the last content
video of the recovery module.

16
00:01:04,531 --> 00:01:08,899
You've recovered commits from reflogs,
from git fsck, from other clones.

17
00:01:08,939 --> 00:01:12,756
A reflog is your clone's own list
of the values a name has had.

18
00:01:12,796 --> 00:01:17,776
Today you walk the ladder in the other
direction, from "a reflog names it" down to

19
00:01:17,816 --> 00:01:22,612
"the object is deleted", one command per
step, so that you can show each state.

20
00:01:22,952 --> 00:01:27,838
Then prevention, which is cheaper than
any recovery: a backup ref before a risky

21
00:01:27,878 --> 00:01:33,010
operation, and a bundle as an offline backup.
And last, the server side: what GitHub

22
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:36,581
adds, described from its
documentation and not from a run.

23
00:01:36,921 --> 00:01:41,659
You'll hear layer two, three and four today.
Those are the four layers of protection

24
00:01:41,699 --> 00:01:45,811
from the start of this module: refs,
reflogs, the grace period, and other

25
00:01:45,851 --> 00:01:46,550
repositories.

26
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:52,390
describe the sequence that destroys the
safety net, and when it is appropriate;

27
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:55,812
show each step from "reflog
protects it" to "pruned";

28
00:01:56,152 --> 00:01:59,246
protect work before a risky
operation with a backup ref;

29
00:01:59,586 --> 00:02:02,892
create, verify and use a
bundle as an offline backup;

30
00:02:03,232 --> 00:02:08,293
say which evidence GitHub keeps on the
server side, as the cited section states it.

31
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:14,338
In one sentence: a lost commit
becomes unrecoverable in two steps.

32
00:02:14,378 --> 00:02:17,746
First the last reflog entry
that names it is removed.

33
00:02:17,786 --> 00:02:21,733
Then a collection, Git's housekeeping
run, deletes the object.

34
00:02:21,773 --> 00:02:26,878
And two commands can force both steps at
once. On screen is today's lost commit,

35
00:02:26,918 --> 00:02:31,058
dbe6ec8: a hard reset moved main
off it, so no branch reaches it.

36
00:02:31,398 --> 00:02:35,733
Precisely. The destructive pair is the
documented procedure for shrinking a

37
00:02:35,773 --> 00:02:40,459
repository after a history rewrite. The
manual of git filter-branch lists it in a

38
00:02:40,499 --> 00:02:43,551
checklist and calls it "a
very destructive approach".

39
00:02:43,591 --> 00:02:47,635
For the same reason it's the documented
way to destroy the safety net.

40
00:02:47,975 --> 00:02:52,569
Inside the dot git folder, step one
truncates files in the logs folder.

41
00:02:52,609 --> 00:02:57,790
Step two rewrites the pack folder under
objects, and removes loose object files.

42
00:02:57,830 --> 00:03:02,946
The working tree, the index, HEAD and the
current branch are unchanged by both.

43
00:03:03,286 --> 00:03:09,674
git reflog expire --expire=now --all. What
it changes: it empties every reflog, the

44
00:03:09,714 --> 00:03:16,024
stash list included. What it can destroy:
every layer-two protection, and the list

45
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:21,910
of all stash entries except the newest.
Preview: git reflog expire --dry-run

46
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:27,563
--verbose. Recovery: the objects still
exist, so git fsck, until a prune.

47
00:03:27,603 --> 00:03:32,675
Appropriate when: a secret must be
purged after a history rewrite.

48
00:03:33,015 --> 00:03:38,776
git gc --prune=now. What it changes: it
repacks and deletes all unreachable objects.

49
00:03:38,816 --> 00:03:44,507
What it can destroy: everything on layer
three. Preview: git prune -n, and git fsck

50
00:03:44,547 --> 00:03:49,065
--unreachable. Recovery: none in
this repository, only layer four.

51
00:03:49,105 --> 00:03:54,383
Appropriate when: the same purge, with
no other process using the repository.

52
00:03:54,423 --> 00:04:00,115
The manual attaches a second warning:
--prune=now "increases the risk of corruption

53
00:04:00,155 --> 00:04:04,396
if another process is writing to
the repository concurrently".

54
00:04:04,436 --> 00:04:09,921
An object that a running command has
written and not yet attached to a ref looks

55
00:04:09,961 --> 00:04:10,750
unreachable.

56
00:04:11,090 --> 00:04:17,001
git prune: deletes unreachable loose objects
with no grace period unless --expire is

57
00:04:17,041 --> 00:04:20,190
given. The manual says
to run git gc instead.

58
00:04:20,530 --> 00:04:25,867
And for contrast, plain git gc is 🟡
CAUTION: it repacks, expires reflogs by the

59
00:04:25,907 --> 00:04:30,836
configured periods, and deletes
unreachable objects older than two weeks.

60
00:04:30,876 --> 00:04:32,265
Routine housekeeping.

61
00:04:32,605 --> 00:04:36,246
Automatic maintenance. You
rarely run git gc yourself.

62
00:04:36,286 --> 00:04:41,903
Some commands start maintenance when they
finish. Since Git 2.54 that automatic run

63
00:04:41,943 --> 00:04:47,493
uses the geometric strategy and not the gc
task. It expires data in the reflog and

64
00:04:47,533 --> 00:04:53,014
puts unreachable objects into a cruft pack,
a pack for unreachable objects with a

65
00:04:53,054 --> 00:04:55,468
file that records each object's age.

66
00:04:55,808 --> 00:05:01,199
Cruft packs are the default from Git 2.41.
The retention periods you learned hold

67
00:05:01,239 --> 00:05:06,630
under either strategy. The manual of git
gc still describes the older trigger, so

68
00:05:06,670 --> 00:05:10,786
read "automatic gc" in older
texts as "automatic maintenance".

69
00:05:11,126 --> 00:05:15,455
Prevention, part one: a backup ref.
A branch costs one small file.

70
00:05:15,495 --> 00:05:21,015
Here's a branch from today's demo, three
commits ahead of main, about to be squashed

71
00:05:21,055 --> 00:05:21,809
by a rebase.

72
00:05:22,149 --> 00:05:27,644
Created before a rebase, a history filter,
a large merge or a reset, a backup branch

73
00:05:27,684 --> 00:05:32,916
turns recovery into one command that needs
no reflog, no search and no deadline.

74
00:05:33,256 --> 00:05:38,114
After the rebase, the branch ends in a new
commit, and the backup still names the

75
00:05:38,154 --> 00:05:42,890
old ones. You compare against a name, not
against a reflog position that moves.

76
00:05:43,230 --> 00:05:48,387
Prevention, part two: a bundle. In one
sentence: git bundle writes refs and the

77
00:05:48,427 --> 00:05:52,795
objects they reach into one file
that Git can clone and fetch from.

78
00:05:52,835 --> 00:05:57,071
Precisely, a bundle is a pack file
with a header that lists refs.

79
00:05:57,111 --> 00:06:02,136
🟢 SAFE: git bundle create <file> --all
includes every ref and writes one file

80
00:06:02,176 --> 00:06:02,992
outside .git.

81
00:06:03,332 --> 00:06:09,041
What a bundle doesn't hold: reflogs, the
index, hooks, the configuration, untracked

82
00:06:09,081 --> 00:06:14,653
files, and of the stash at most the newest
entry. For stashes, Git 2.51 added git

83
00:06:14,693 --> 00:06:20,402
stash export and git stash import. A copy
of the whole directory, made while no Git

84
00:06:20,442 --> 00:06:24,074
command runs, holds everything,
and verifies nothing.

85
00:06:24,114 --> 00:06:29,754
For a repository you can't afford to lose,
use both kinds, and run git fsck on the

86
00:06:29,794 --> 00:06:30,101
copy.

87
00:06:31,941 --> 00:06:35,862
Return to the vault one last time, and
picture a ladder with three rungs.

88
00:06:36,202 --> 00:06:40,571
On the top rung, the ledger mentions
the box. You recover it by name.

89
00:06:40,611 --> 00:06:43,893
On the middle rung, the
ledger line has been erased.

90
00:06:43,933 --> 00:06:47,918
The box is on the shelf, unmentioned.
You recover it by search.

91
00:06:48,258 --> 00:06:51,386
On the bottom rung, the
clear-out has emptied the box.

92
00:06:51,426 --> 00:06:53,615
There's nothing in this vault to find.

93
00:06:53,955 --> 00:06:58,952
Normally time and maintenance take a box
down the ladder slowly: 30 or 90 days for

94
00:06:58,992 --> 00:07:03,375
the first step, two weeks of object
age and a collection for the second.

95
00:07:03,415 --> 00:07:06,693
The destructive pair kicks
it down both steps at once.

96
00:07:07,033 --> 00:07:12,364
A backup ref is an index card you write
before the risky operation, so the box never

97
00:07:12,404 --> 00:07:17,608
leaves the front desk. A bundle is a sealed
copy of the boxes and cards, stored in

98
00:07:17,648 --> 00:07:22,596
another building. The analogy breaks at
one point: the sealed copy contains no

99
00:07:22,636 --> 00:07:24,643
ledger. A bundle has no reflogs.

100
00:07:26,483 --> 00:07:31,625
Try it now, on paper. Pause me for thirty
seconds and draw the ladder from memory:

101
00:07:31,665 --> 00:07:35,038
three states, two arrows
down. Then check your answer.

102
00:07:39,878 --> 00:07:44,862
Each arrow down has two labels: the command
that forces the step, and the slow path

103
00:07:44,902 --> 00:07:49,766
that takes it by default. Each state has,
on its right, the tool that still works

104
00:07:49,806 --> 00:07:50,129
there.

105
00:07:51,969 --> 00:07:57,771
Top rung. Now the first step down,
in a second copy of the repository.

106
00:07:57,811 --> 00:08:03,780
The five answers for git reflog expire
--expire=now --all are on screen.

107
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:06,952
git prune -n is 🟢 SAFE: it only lists.

108
00:08:07,292 --> 00:08:11,882
Predict how many objects an immediate
prune would delete for one lost commit.

109
00:08:11,922 --> 00:08:13,506
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

110
00:08:23,346 --> 00:08:27,616
Three: the commit, its tree, and the
one blob that no other commit shares.

111
00:08:27,656 --> 00:08:32,508
git fsck now reports the commit as dangling.
Middle rung: it's still fully readable.

112
00:08:32,848 --> 00:08:37,433
A collection that respects a grace
period doesn't delete such objects.

113
00:08:37,473 --> 00:08:42,125
The transcript uses --prune=never so
that it doesn't depend on a clock.

114
00:08:42,165 --> 00:08:46,816
A plain git gc behaves the same way
for objects younger than two weeks.

115
00:08:47,156 --> 00:08:51,847
Twelve objects in two packs. One of the
packs has a file with the extension mtimes:

116
00:08:51,887 --> 00:08:54,640
that's the cruft pack. The
commit is still there.

117
00:08:56,980 --> 00:09:02,547
Before the next command, the five answers
for git gc --prune=now once more, aloud.

118
00:09:02,587 --> 00:09:07,608
It deletes all unreachable objects. It
destroys everything on layer three.

119
00:09:07,648 --> 00:09:13,010
The preview was git prune -n, which you
have read. Recovery in this repository:

120
00:09:13,050 --> 00:09:18,618
none. Appropriate: a deliberate purge, with
no other process using the repository.

121
00:09:18,658 --> 00:09:21,353
This is a sandbox built for the purpose.

122
00:09:21,693 --> 00:09:25,250
Nine objects in one pack.
"Not a valid object name".

123
00:09:25,290 --> 00:09:30,714
And git fsck reports nothing: the repository
is healthy, and the commit doesn't

124
00:09:30,754 --> 00:09:32,028
exist. Bottom rung.

125
00:09:43,668 --> 00:09:48,423
833b96f went from a reflog entry,
to a dangling commit, to nothing.

126
00:09:48,463 --> 00:09:53,576
Now the proof. Quick quiz first. The
file ORIG_HEAD still holds that ID.

127
00:09:53,616 --> 00:09:59,158
Option one: so Git can still read the
commit. Option two: the ID is there, the

128
00:09:59,198 --> 00:10:01,305
object isn't. Say it out loud.

129
00:10:06,145 --> 00:10:09,683
Option two. The reflog is
empty. git fsck finds nothing.

130
00:10:09,723 --> 00:10:14,923
ORIG_HEAD still holds the ID of the deleted
commit and resolves to nothing: it's a

131
00:10:14,963 --> 00:10:20,036
file, not a starting point for reachability.
And you can't create a branch at an

132
00:10:20,076 --> 00:10:21,697
object that doesn't exist.

133
00:10:22,037 --> 00:10:26,934
What can still bring the commit back? That
was the opening question, and the answer

134
00:10:26,974 --> 00:10:30,206
is: only layer four. The lab
kept a bundle from before.

135
00:10:37,746 --> 00:10:42,694
A fetch from a file. The commit is back,
with the same ID, because the ID is

136
00:10:42,734 --> 00:10:44,400
computed from the content.

137
00:10:44,740 --> 00:10:48,885
One command before the rebase. Now
use the name to check the result.

138
00:10:49,225 --> 00:10:54,279
The empty git diff --stat proves that the
squashed branch has the same content as

139
00:10:54,319 --> 00:10:57,737
the original. git range-diff
shows how the commits map.

140
00:10:57,777 --> 00:11:02,642
Had the result been wrong, one reset to
the backup name would undo it, with no

141
00:11:02,682 --> 00:11:07,861
reflog involved. The script runs it to show
that. Delete the backup when the result

142
00:11:07,901 --> 00:11:09,308
is pushed and verified.

143
00:11:09,648 --> 00:11:12,938
"is okay", five refs,
"records a complete history".

144
00:11:12,978 --> 00:11:15,681
Verify every bundle you intend to rely on.

145
00:11:16,021 --> 00:11:18,201
A clone from a file, with the full graph.

146
00:11:18,541 --> 00:11:24,008
Git gives a server no reflog by default,
and you can't run git reflog or git fsck on

147
00:11:24,048 --> 00:11:29,055
GitHub's copy of your repository. The
section lists GitHub's own instruments.

148
00:11:29,095 --> 00:11:33,316
Open your own practice repository
and find each one as I name it.

149
00:11:33,656 --> 00:11:38,510
The Activity view of a repository: pushes,
force pushes, merges, branch creations

150
00:11:38,550 --> 00:11:43,464
and deletions, with the user and a comparison.
A force push is a push that removes

151
00:11:43,504 --> 00:11:48,418
commits from a branch on the server. "Restore
branch" on a closed pull request: it

152
00:11:48,458 --> 00:11:53,069
recreates the deleted head branch, only
for branches that had a pull request.

153
00:11:53,409 --> 00:11:58,884
The Events API: PushEvent records whose
before and head fields are the IDs on both

154
00:11:58,924 --> 00:12:04,331
sides of a push. The section gives its
limits as the last 300 events and 30 days,

155
00:12:04,371 --> 00:12:09,845
with a delivery lag of 30 seconds to 6
hours. The Git references API: it creates a

156
00:12:09,885 --> 00:12:15,225
ref at a commit ID you supply, and works
only while GitHub still has the commit.

157
00:12:15,265 --> 00:12:20,403
And the audit log: organization events
for 180 days, while Git events such as

158
00:12:20,443 --> 00:12:24,169
git.push are in the enterprise
log only, for seven days.

159
00:12:24,509 --> 00:12:29,207
The recipe these pieces suggest is to read
the ID from before the force push and

160
00:12:29,247 --> 00:12:30,509
create a branch at it:

161
00:12:33,349 --> 00:12:38,094
That recipe is unverified. GitHub documents
each instrument, not this combined

162
00:12:38,134 --> 00:12:43,002
procedure, and it publishes no retention
period for commits that no ref reaches.

163
00:12:43,042 --> 00:12:48,093
One group of researchers observed that such
commits appear to be kept indefinitely.

164
00:12:48,133 --> 00:12:51,345
That is their observation,
not a statement by GitHub.

165
00:12:51,685 --> 00:12:56,738
GitHub's own advice for a deleted or
force-pushed branch is the plain Git route: ask

166
00:12:56,778 --> 00:13:00,800
a collaborator who still has the
commit to push it to a new branch.

167
00:13:00,840 --> 00:13:05,651
The same persistence means that a force
push doesn't remove a leaked secret from

168
00:13:05,691 --> 00:13:10,683
GitHub. Rotation is the remedy. Prevention
on this layer is a ruleset, a named list

169
00:13:10,723 --> 00:13:15,412
of rules on GitHub, that blocks force
pushes and deletions on shared branches.

170
00:13:17,252 --> 00:13:18,900
Five mistakes to watch for.

171
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:24,023
Running the destructive pair as "clean-up".
Root cause: step one removes every

172
00:13:24,063 --> 00:13:29,216
reflog entry and step two deletes every
object that nothing names; together they are

173
00:13:29,256 --> 00:13:31,936
the documented way to
remove the safety net.

174
00:13:32,276 --> 00:13:37,176
Relying on ORIG_HEAD to keep a commit alive.
Root cause: it is one file with one ID

175
00:13:37,216 --> 00:13:40,211
and is not a starting point
for garbage collection.

176
00:13:40,551 --> 00:13:44,599
Running git gc --prune=now while
another Git process is working.

177
00:13:44,639 --> 00:13:49,838
Root cause: an object that a running command
has written and not yet attached to a

178
00:13:49,878 --> 00:13:53,543
ref looks unreachable, and the
manual warns of corruption.

179
00:13:53,883 --> 00:13:57,093
Treating a bundle as a full
copy of the repository.

180
00:13:57,133 --> 00:14:02,191
Root cause: a bundle holds what its refs
reach; it has no reflogs, index, hooks,

181
00:14:02,231 --> 00:14:04,294
configuration or untracked files.

182
00:14:04,634 --> 00:14:07,666
Assuming a force push removed
a commit from GitHub.

183
00:14:07,706 --> 00:14:12,365
Root cause: the server's retention of
unreferenced commits is not published; a

184
00:14:12,405 --> 00:14:15,677
leaked secret is handled by
rotation, not by rewriting.

185
00:14:17,517 --> 00:14:22,000
Now, out of the lab. An ML team is
about to rewrite the history of a model

186
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:26,278
repository to remove a large checkpoint
that was committed by mistake.

187
00:14:26,318 --> 00:14:30,617
Experiment logs and model cards refer
to commit IDs of the old history.

188
00:14:30,957 --> 00:14:34,867
Before the rewrite, the lead does
two things from section 13.14.

189
00:14:34,907 --> 00:14:39,927
She creates a backup ref at the old tip.
And she stores a bundle of the old state,

190
00:14:39,967 --> 00:14:44,987
verified, with the incident record: it
preserves every old commit ID that the logs

191
00:14:45,027 --> 00:14:50,108
and cards refer to. Only after the rewrite
is pushed and verified does the team run

192
00:14:50,148 --> 00:14:55,292
the destructive pair in each clone.
Deliberately, with no other Git process running,

193
00:14:55,332 --> 00:14:58,377
because here shrinking the
repository is the goal.

194
00:14:58,717 --> 00:15:03,426
The team's rule for everyday work is
shorter: experiment branches that live for

195
00:15:03,466 --> 00:15:06,792
months are pushed, not left
to the reflog of one laptop.

196
00:15:08,632 --> 00:15:13,930
Your turn. Do Lab 12.12, "Prove
the point of no return", in

197
00:15:13,970 --> 00:15:16,373
lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

198
00:15:16,713 --> 00:15:21,581
At each rung, before you run the command
that takes the commit one step down,

199
00:15:21,621 --> 00:15:26,808
predict the output of git reflog, of git
fsck, and of git cat-file -t for the lost

200
00:15:26,848 --> 00:15:31,907
commit. At the bottom, list every tool of
this module and predict what each will

201
00:15:31,947 --> 00:15:33,373
say, then try them all.

202
00:15:33,713 --> 00:15:40,196
The challenge is Exercise 12.11, Level
5, "Deleted, then cleaned up", in

203
00:15:40,236 --> 00:15:44,183
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

204
00:15:46,023 --> 00:15:47,953
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

205
00:15:52,793 --> 00:15:57,448
A strong answer takes the two commands
separately and says for each what is removed

206
00:15:57,488 --> 00:16:02,030
and what is left untouched, including refs,
the working tree and the newest stash

